{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjEGhK6iyeZ9XdJNsXnd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QZrVqBG4AjRHto8AC1ELPz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211953__xee4d12/work/PLAN.md","offset":300,"limit":130},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18749,"cache_read_input_tokens":596441,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18749},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23de6e6b-3ae6-4bf5-9764-44b18bb5857c","uuid":"3efaece9-c9c2-42e4-a06b-d9cf38d838c2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:28:58.195Z","request_id":"req_011CgjEGgzVVirYWfNTZ3w73","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QZrVqBG4AjRHto8AC1ELPz":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211953__xee4d12/work/PLAN.md","offset":300,"limit":130}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QZrVqBG4AjRHto8AC1ELPz","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t  window.__game = { game, get world() { return world; }, get hero() { return hero; } };\n301\t}\n302\tmain();\n303\t```\n304\t\n305\t### 3.6 Facts and traps\n306\t\n307\tEach of these cost the author time, or would have.\n308\t\n309\t**Testing the 3D**\n310\t- `engine/tools/check.mjs` and `filmstrip.mjs` read the clear 2D canvas, so on a real-3D game they **falsely fail** (\"the screen stayed one flat color\"). Don't use them or act on their look notes. Use your own runner (§17) and `page.screenshot()`.\n311\t- Headless Chromium defaults to WebGL 2 here, which is what most tests should use.\n312\t- **Real players run WebGPU, so test it too.** Plain headless WebGPU loses its device (`Instance dropped in popErrorScope`, `Device Lost`) because headless can't present. Use `standInCanvas` and the launch args from `engine/tools/kit3d-test.mjs` (around lines 247 to 283): add the function with `addInitScript`. With it, zone swaps ran clean and `__readFrame(2)` returned a real PNG.\n313\t- Headless Firefox has no WebGL. Run it headed under `xvfb-run` with `firefox_user_prefs: { 'webgl.force-enabled': true }`.\n314\t\n315\t**The picture**\n316\t- A custom `W3.SIDE` or `W3.GROUND` function must return `[r, g, b]` (use `E.hex('#...')`). A hex string bakes **pure black**.\n317\t- `{ wall: 14, side: 'plaster', platform: 48, thick: 16 }` on one tile makes a window: a sill and a lintel, with an opening you can see and shoot through.\n318\t- Doors are floor-tile gaps with a `world.thing('door', ...)` in them. Roofs are `E.SHAPES` entries placed on top of the walls with `world.shape`.\n319\t- `world.reset()` removes everything placed with `world.shape()` (legend shapes stay). Place them again after a reset.\n320\t- The ground bakes into one 2048-pixel texture, so zones up to **128 × 128 tiles** keep full texture detail.\n321\t- `outlines: false` plus `camera.target` set about 20 m ahead of the eye gives the clean look and centres the sun's shadow where the player looks. With outlines on, pen bodies look heavy and blobby.\n322\t- World options reserve lights up front: `lights: n` for `world.light`, `spots: n` for `world.flashlight`.\n323\t- World3D's rules from its manual: WebGPURenderer and node materials only; no `ShaderMaterial`, `EffectComposer` or addons. Make meshes and lights when the world is made, not mid-game. An InstancedMesh over 1,000 instances never uses `DynamicDrawUsage`.\n324\t- Pens empty after every frame: draw in the world's `onDraw`, whichever scene is showing. Pen batches grow by themselves, so crowd size has no cap.\n325\t\n326\t**Levels and movement**\n327\t- One walkable height per tile. `{ floor: h }` is solid down to the ground, so \"upstairs\" exists only above something solid: a mezzanine at the back of a house, a balcony ring over pillars. Platform slabs can be walked on but are not in the flow grid.\n328\t- `flow` climbs stairs and drops off ledges but never jumps, climbs ladders, goes through windows or knows about door things. Those are **links** (§8). One `world.flow()` per target; `flow.to` is cheap while the target stays on one tile.\n329\t- `a.place(x, y, z)` moves an actor regardless of collision. Scripted traversal (vault, ladder, drop, grab, cutscene marks) moves the actor with `place` each step.\n330\t- `E.Body`, `Platformer` and the 2D `TileMap` are not used. The 2D engine still runs scenes, input, menus, saves, sound and the HUD.\n331\t\n332\t**Input**\n333\t- `game.input.padAim` is the right stick, `world.camera.takeMouse(out)` the captured mouse, `game.input.value('fire')` a trigger's travel.\n334\t- `input.label(a)` follows the last device, but it treats keyboard and mouse as two devices, so a prompt would flip between `E` and `Left click`. Route every prompt through one helper (§13).\n335\t\n336\t**Clips**\n337\t- Body points are millimetres; `MM = .016` converts them to engine units (16 units a metre).\n338\t- Clip names repeat across sets, so name them `SET:Clip` when cutting.\n339\t- CMU clips carry lead-in and tail (`Roundhouse_Kick` is 6 s for one kick). Find the strike's window and play only that range.\n340\t\n341\t**Determinism**\n342\t- Gameplay randomness comes from `E.rng(seed)` (in a hub round, `AIHub.rng(label)`). Never `Math.random` in rules.\n343\t- The same inputs then give the same `world.hash()`: that is your determinism test and your replay check.\n344\t- Build the game so `step(dt, ctl)` runs headless inside a page (§4). The horror kit's bot played a whole game of 12,000 steps in about 1.2 s this way.\n345\t\n346\t## 4. Architecture\n347\t\n348\tA spine, then content. Content never edits the core (the engine's own lesson from its biggest game; see \"Building a big game\" in `engine/AI_GUIDE.md`).\n349\t\n350\t- **Registries as a language:** `def('enemy' | 'weapon' | 'item' | 'zone' | 'boss' | 'song' | 'line', id, spec)`. Weapons, enemies, items, prices and zones are plain data in their own files. Someone with a text editor can add a zone, enemy or gun without touching systems. That's what TASK.md means by \"could later be built out with normal designer tools\".\n351\t- **One `Run` object** holds everything a save needs, as ids and numbers only: chapter, zone, spawn, flags, dead and taken ids per zone, hero and ally state, case layout, weapons and levels, crowns, stats, difficulty, tension. `Run.toSave()` and `Run.fromSave()` are the only way in or out.\n352\t- **One rule path:** `step(dt, ctl)` is the only function that changes gameplay. `ctl` is `{ move: [x, y], look: [dx, dy], stick2, down(a), pressed(a), use(a) }`. A human's `ctl` reads `game.input`. A bot's comes from a script. A remote player's comes from network messages. The attract mode and tests take the same path as the player.\n353\t- **Pose in update, draw reads the pose.** Bodies pose in the fixed step, not in draw (the kit poses in draw; move it). Hit tests then run against real joints in headless runs, and replays stay exact. Steps are 1/120 s, so it stays smooth.\n354\t- **An event bus:** `emit('kill' | 'hit' | 'pickup' | 'stamp' | ...)`. Stats, the Director, score, audio and coverage listen, rather than being wired through the combat code.\n355\t- **Coverage:** `FEATURES` in `src/core/features.js` lists every mechanic (§12.2), and code marks `Run.coverage[id]` when one happens. The scripted playthrough must touch them all. That is how you prove the demo shows ALL features.\n356\t- **Tune:** every number a dev slider touches lives in one `Tune` table that systems read each step (§15).\n357\t- **Scenes:** `title`, `play` (campaign zones), `attract`, `laststand`, `card` (hub card), `credits`. Screens over `play` (case, trolley, map, passport, dev) are modes inside the scene that freeze the world, as the horror kit's item screen does.\n358\t- **Zones:** one `World3D` per zone, built on entry behind a fade and a chapter card, and disposed on exit **[verified with the §3.4 patch: about 0.4 s even on a software renderer]**. Each zone's data sets its own light, fog and sky. Boot builds only what the first screen needs. In a hub session, build only the arena.\n359\t- **Saves and checkpoints:** autosave at each zone entry and before each boss (`game.saves.autosave()`); stamp desks write the three slots (`game.saves.write = () => ({ data: Run.toSave(), place, level })`). Death offers Continue from the latest. Title's Continue uses `saves.latest()`. Keep a `version` in the save. The attract mode, tests' bots and hub rounds never write saves.\n360\t- **Debug handle:** `window.__game = { game, Run, step, bots, ... }` and `game.debug = { snapshot() {...} }`.\n361\t\n362\t## 5. Bodies and animation\n363\t\n364\tTASK.md: use the engine's native animation system as the base, and make new animations for what makes the game feel like the source. **[verified]** The engine's puppet `E.Humanoid` looks crude this close to the camera (ball head, no visible gun) and its cards look coarse. The two pipelines below are also engine-native and hold up over the shoulder, so use these instead.\n365\t\n366\t**Pipeline A, soldier skeleton (anyone who holds a gun: Vera, Sister Perpetua, Vane Security, Last Stand characters).** Copy `v3..spring`, `body`, `settle`, `pose`, `gunFrame`, `handTargets`, `deadPose`, `drawGun`, `muzzleFlash` and `reloadCurve` from the shooter kit (lines 648 to 1054). You get planted IK feet, a spine that leans and twists, hands on the gun, aim, recoil, reload, ladder and ledge poses, a kick, hit springs and a death fold. Then:\n367\t- Write your own `drawVera`, `drawGuard` and so on after `drawSoldier`'s pattern: slabs and tubes over `J`.\n368\t- Add pistol, magnum and SMG to `drawGun`.\n369\t- Give `pose()` a no-helmet head and a shoulder swap.\n370\t- **Vera's back is on screen for the whole game.** Give it the most care: a navy windbreaker with EXCISE in yellow block letters (a small plane mesh with a baked canvas texture, made with the world and placed on her chest frame, or slabs), a dark bob. Hide her body when the camera is pulled closer than about 12 units.\n371\t\n372\t**Pipeline B, clip bodies (melee enemies, Ambrose, the Duty-Free Man, villains, cutscene actors).** `lib.sample(clip, t, buf)` gives 36 named body points, and a drawer draws limbs over them with the pen (`drawBody` in §3.5 is the template; the look is **[verified]**).\n373\t- Use `lib.blend(a, b, k)` to crossfade clips over about 0.15 s.\n374\t- Add a small lean spring on the chest and head for hit reactions, so a shot visibly rocks them.\n375\t- Scale playback to ground speed so feet don't skate: `t += dt * speed / clipSpeed`.\n376\t- Actors are invisible physics capsules: `world.mob({ visible: false, shadow: false })` or `world.hero(...)`. The pen's parts cast shadows by themselves.\n377\t- `a.setSolid(false)` when they die, so the crowd walks over them.\n378\t\n379\t**Variety.** One drawer per archetype, many looks: palette sets, then hats (flat cap, headscarf, straw hat, wicker basket), aprons, tools in the right fist (pitchfork, pruning hook, sickle, mattock, vine shears), wine stains, a Bloom growth on neck or shoulder. Seed each from its id so a save restores the same villager. Giants: scale the point positions (`MM * k`) and limb radii.\n380\t\n381\t**Which clip for what** (all in the §3.5 set):\n382\t\n383\t| Need | Clip |\n384\t|---|---|\n385\t| Shamble, idle, limp | `Zombie_Walk_Loop`, `Zombie_Walk_Fwd_Loop`, `Zombie_Idle_Loop`, `Wounded_Walk_Loop`, `Hurt_Stomach_Walk_Loop` |\n386\t| Overhead tool, hook swing, claw or grab, throw | `Chop_Wood`, `Melee_Hook`, `Zombie_Scratch`, `OverhandThrow` |\n387\t| Stagger (KICK window), kneel (SLAM window) | `Dizzy`, `Kneeling Tired` |\n388\t| Hit, thrown down, get up, die | `Hit_Chest`, `Hit_Head`, `Hit_Knockback`, `LayToIdle`, `Fall_On_Face`, `Get_Up_Face_Down`, `Death01`, `Death_A/B/C` |\n389\t| Monk shield, Steward lunge | `Idle_Shield_Loop`, `Idle_Shield_Break`, `Sword_Dash_RM` |\n390\t| Vera's kick | `Roundhouse_Kick`, `Side_Kick`, `Front_Kick` (windows only) |\n391\t| Traversal | `Climb Ladder`, `ClimbUp_1m_RM`, `Run_Jump_Over`, `Land_Three_Point`, `Push_Loop`, `Crawl` (downed) |\n392\t| Ambrose | `Walk_Formal_Loop`, `Idle_FoldArms_Loop`, `Defend`, `Duck_Thrown_Object`, `Drink_Water`, `Consume`, `Greeting` |\n393\t| Carrying him | `Walk_Carry_Loop`, `Carry_Heavy_Box` |\n394\t| Talk, banquet, balcony, lantern patrol | `Idle_Talking_Loop`, `Sitting_Idle_Loop`, `Sitting_Talking_Loop`, `Idle_Rail_Loop`, `Idle_Lantern_Loop`, `Looking_Around` |\n395\t\n396\t**New animation we must author** (a clip is text; see `engine/docs/MOCAP.md`; copy the nearest clip's key poses and edit the numbers, or add a procedural layer):\n397\t- **SLAM:** Vera behind a kneeling enemy, hooks under the arms and throws them back over her hip. Two-body sync: paired clips, positions placed by script.\n398\t- **FRISK:** a quick pat-down of a downed enemy that shakes loose crowns.\n399\t- **Stomp and heel-grind** (finish a crawler or a downed enemy).\n400\t- **Ambrose's tasting** (from `Consume`: sniff, swirl, sip, grimace) and **Ambrose carried** over a shoulder (a limp hung pose).\n401\t- **Boost and catch** (two-body sync).\n402\t- **The Treader's stomp and cask throw**, **the Comte's languid gestures** on the sedan chair, **Vane's transformation** (pieces drawn procedurally).\n403\t- **The sabrage** at the finale.\n404\t\n405\tCheck each once with a strip of screenshots stepped by `game.advance` (§17).\n406\t\n407\t**Non-human bodies** (draw with the pen on a small procedural skeleton; the horror kit's `drawDog` is the pattern): truffle hog, Bloom crawler, Bloom tendril (a chain of tubes on a spring), the Comte's vine colossus, Vane's final form.\n408\t\n409\t**Hit zones.** For each enemy near the shot's line, build capsules from its pose: head (a sphere at the skull, about 3 units), torso (pelvis to neck, about 4), upper and lower arm, thigh and shin. The shot:\n410\t1. `world.ray(ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz, range, { skip: [hero, ...enemies] })` finds what the level or props stop it at.\n411\t2. Test the ray against the capsules, nearest first, up to that distance. The first capsule hit is the zone.\n412\t\n413\tKeep capsules a little fatter than the art at range, and let aim assist (§6) help the pad player.\n414\t\n415\t## 6. The player\n416\t\n417\t**Controls.** Bind them in `KEYS` with these actions and name them all for the engine's rebinding screen (`menu.controls`). DualSense standard mapping. Keyboard and mouse are what PC players expect, not stand-ins.\n418\t\n419\t| Action | DualSense | Keyboard / mouse |\n420\t|---|---|---|\n421\t| Move / look | Left stick / right stick | WASD / mouse (a click captures it) |\n422\t| Aim (hold; feet planted) | L2 | Right mouse |\n423\t| Fire | R2 | Left mouse (while aiming) |\n424\t| Action: door, vault, ladder, pick up, talk, KICK, SLAM, prompts | Cross | E (also Space), or left mouse on the focused thing when not aiming |\n425\t| Sprint (hold); sprint + Action kicks a door in | Circle (also Cancel in menus) | Shift |\n426\t| Quick turn (180°) | Stick back + Cross | X, or S + Space |\n427\t| Reload | Square | R |\n428\t| Evidence case | Triangle | Tab or I |\n429\t| Knife (a tap slashes) | L1 | F |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23de6e6b-3ae6-4bf5-9764-44b18bb5857c","uuid":"71243563-49af-4b4e-b385-a879d1d14a13","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:28:58.202Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211953__xee4d12/work/PLAN.md","content":"  window.__game = { game, get world() { return world; }, get hero() { return hero; } };\n}\nmain();\n```\n\n### 3.6 Facts and traps\n\nEach of these cost the author time, or would have.\n\n**Testing the 3D**\n- `engine/tools/check.mjs` and `filmstrip.mjs` read the clear 2D canvas, so on a real-3D game they **falsely fail** (\"the screen stayed one flat color\"). Don't use them or act on their look notes. Use your own runner (§17) and `page.screenshot()`.\n- Headless Chromium defaults to WebGL 2 here, which is what most tests should use.\n- **Real players run WebGPU, so test it too.** Plain headless WebGPU loses its device (`Instance dropped in popErrorScope`, `Device Lost`) because headless can't present. Use `standInCanvas` and the launch args from `engine/tools/kit3d-test.mjs` (around lines 247 to 283): add the function with `addInitScript`. With it, zone swaps ran clean and `__readFrame(2)` returned a real PNG.\n- Headless Firefox has no WebGL. Run it headed under `xvfb-run` with `firefox_user_prefs: { 'webgl.force-enabled': true }`.\n\n**The picture**\n- A custom `W3.SIDE` or `W3.GROUND` function must return `[r, g, b]` (use `E.hex('#...')`). A hex string bakes **pure black**.\n- `{ wall: 14, side: 'plaster', platform: 48, thick: 16 }` on one tile makes a window: a sill and a lintel, with an opening you can see and shoot through.\n- Doors are floor-tile gaps with a `world.thing('door', ...)` in them. Roofs are `E.SHAPES` entries placed on top of the walls with `world.shape`.\n- `world.reset()` removes everything placed with `world.shape()` (legend shapes stay). Place them again after a reset.\n- The ground bakes into one 2048-pixel texture, so zones up to **128 × 128 tiles** keep full texture detail.\n- `outlines: false` plus `camera.target` set about 20 m ahead of the eye gives the clean look and centres the sun's shadow where the player looks. With outlines on, pen bodies look heavy and blobby.\n- World options reserve lights up front: `lights: n` for `world.light`, `spots: n` for `world.flashlight`.\n- World3D's rules from its manual: WebGPURenderer and node materials only; no `ShaderMaterial`, `EffectComposer` or addons. Make meshes and lights when the world is made, not mid-game. An InstancedMesh over 1,000 instances never uses `DynamicDrawUsage`.\n- Pens empty after every frame: draw in the world's `onDraw`, whichever scene is showing. Pen batches grow by themselves, so crowd size has no cap.\n\n**Levels and movement**\n- One walkable height per tile. `{ floor: h }` is solid down to the ground, so \"upstairs\" exists only above something solid: a mezzanine at the back of a house, a balcony ring over pillars. Platform slabs can be walked on but are not in the flow grid.\n- `flow` climbs stairs and drops off ledges but never jumps, climbs ladders, goes through windows or knows about door things. Those are **links** (§8). One `world.flow()` per target; `flow.to` is cheap while the target stays on one tile.\n- `a.place(x, y, z)` moves an actor regardless of collision. Scripted traversal (vault, ladder, drop, grab, cutscene marks) moves the actor with `place` each step.\n- `E.Body`, `Platformer` and the 2D `TileMap` are not used. The 2D engine still runs scenes, input, menus, saves, sound and the HUD.\n\n**Input**\n- `game.input.padAim` is the right stick, `world.camera.takeMouse(out)` the captured mouse, `game.input.value('fire')` a trigger's travel.\n- `input.label(a)` follows the last device, but it treats keyboard and mouse as two devices, so a prompt would flip between `E` and `Left click`. Route every prompt through one helper (§13).\n\n**Clips**\n- Body points are millimetres; `MM = .016` converts them to engine units (16 units a metre).\n- Clip names repeat across sets, so name them `SET:Clip` when cutting.\n- CMU clips carry lead-in and tail (`Roundhouse_Kick` is 6 s for one kick). Find the strike's window and play only that range.\n\n**Determinism**\n- Gameplay randomness comes from `E.rng(seed)` (in a hub round, `AIHub.rng(label)`). Never `Math.random` in rules.\n- The same inputs then give the same `world.hash()`: that is your determinism test and your replay check.\n- Build the game so `step(dt, ctl)` runs headless inside a page (§4). The horror kit's bot played a whole game of 12,000 steps in about 1.2 s this way.\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\nA spine, then content. Content never edits the core (the engine's own lesson from its biggest game; see \"Building a big game\" in `engine/AI_GUIDE.md`).\n\n- **Registries as a language:** `def('enemy' | 'weapon' | 'item' | 'zone' | 'boss' | 'song' | 'line', id, spec)`. Weapons, enemies, items, prices and zones are plain data in their own files. Someone with a text editor can add a zone, enemy or gun without touching systems. That's what TASK.md means by \"could later be built out with normal designer tools\".\n- **One `Run` object** holds everything a save needs, as ids and numbers only: chapter, zone, spawn, flags, dead and taken ids per zone, hero and ally state, case layout, weapons and levels, crowns, stats, difficulty, tension. `Run.toSave()` and `Run.fromSave()` are the only way in or out.\n- **One rule path:** `step(dt, ctl)` is the only function that changes gameplay. `ctl` is `{ move: [x, y], look: [dx, dy], stick2, down(a), pressed(a), use(a) }`. A human's `ctl` reads `game.input`. A bot's comes from a script. A remote player's comes from network messages. The attract mode and tests take the same path as the player.\n- **Pose in update, draw reads the pose.** Bodies pose in the fixed step, not in draw (the kit poses in draw; move it). Hit tests then run against real joints in headless runs, and replays stay exact. Steps are 1/120 s, so it stays smooth.\n- **An event bus:** `emit('kill' | 'hit' | 'pickup' | 'stamp' | ...)`. Stats, the Director, score, audio and coverage listen, rather than being wired through the combat code.\n- **Coverage:** `FEATURES` in `src/core/features.js` lists every mechanic (§12.2), and code marks `Run.coverage[id]` when one happens. The scripted playthrough must touch them all. That is how you prove the demo shows ALL features.\n- **Tune:** every number a dev slider touches lives in one `Tune` table that systems read each step (§15).\n- **Scenes:** `title`, `play` (campaign zones), `attract`, `laststand`, `card` (hub card), `credits`. Screens over `play` (case, trolley, map, passport, dev) are modes inside the scene that freeze the world, as the horror kit's item screen does.\n- **Zones:** one `World3D` per zone, built on entry behind a fade and a chapter card, and disposed on exit **[verified with the §3.4 patch: about 0.4 s even on a software renderer]**. Each zone's data sets its own light, fog and sky. Boot builds only what the first screen needs. In a hub session, build only the arena.\n- **Saves and checkpoints:** autosave at each zone entry and before each boss (`game.saves.autosave()`); stamp desks write the three slots (`game.saves.write = () => ({ data: Run.toSave(), place, level })`). Death offers Continue from the latest. Title's Continue uses `saves.latest()`. Keep a `version` in the save. The attract mode, tests' bots and hub rounds never write saves.\n- **Debug handle:** `window.__game = { game, Run, step, bots, ... }` and `game.debug = { snapshot() {...} }`.\n\n## 5. Bodies and animation\n\nTASK.md: use the engine's native animation system as the base, and make new animations for what makes the game feel like the source. **[verified]** The engine's puppet `E.Humanoid` looks crude this close to the camera (ball head, no visible gun) and its cards look coarse. The two pipelines below are also engine-native and hold up over the shoulder, so use these instead.\n\n**Pipeline A, soldier skeleton (anyone who holds a gun: Vera, Sister Perpetua, Vane Security, Last Stand characters).** Copy `v3..spring`, `body`, `settle`, `pose`, `gunFrame`, `handTargets`, `deadPose`, `drawGun`, `muzzleFlash` and `reloadCurve` from the shooter kit (lines 648 to 1054). You get planted IK feet, a spine that leans and twists, hands on the gun, aim, recoil, reload, ladder and ledge poses, a kick, hit springs and a death fold. Then:\n- Write your own `drawVera`, `drawGuard` and so on after `drawSoldier`'s pattern: slabs and tubes over `J`.\n- Add pistol, magnum and SMG to `drawGun`.\n- Give `pose()` a no-helmet head and a shoulder swap.\n- **Vera's back is on screen for the whole game.** Give it the most care: a navy windbreaker with EXCISE in yellow block letters (a small plane mesh with a baked canvas texture, made with the world and placed on her chest frame, or slabs), a dark bob. Hide her body when the camera is pulled closer than about 12 units.\n\n**Pipeline B, clip bodies (melee enemies, Ambrose, the Duty-Free Man, villains, cutscene actors).** `lib.sample(clip, t, buf)` gives 36 named body points, and a drawer draws limbs over them with the pen (`drawBody` in §3.5 is the template; the look is **[verified]**).\n- Use `lib.blend(a, b, k)` to crossfade clips over about 0.15 s.\n- Add a small lean spring on the chest and head for hit reactions, so a shot visibly rocks them.\n- Scale playback to ground speed so feet don't skate: `t += dt * speed / clipSpeed`.\n- Actors are invisible physics capsules: `world.mob({ visible: false, shadow: false })` or `world.hero(...)`. The pen's parts cast shadows by themselves.\n- `a.setSolid(false)` when they die, so the crowd walks over them.\n\n**Variety.** One drawer per archetype, many looks: palette sets, then hats (flat cap, headscarf, straw hat, wicker basket), aprons, tools in the right fist (pitchfork, pruning hook, sickle, mattock, vine shears), wine stains, a Bloom growth on neck or shoulder. Seed each from its id so a save restores the same villager. Giants: scale the point positions (`MM * k`) and limb radii.\n\n**Which clip for what** (all in the §3.5 set):\n\n| Need | Clip |\n|---|---|\n| Shamble, idle, limp | `Zombie_Walk_Loop`, `Zombie_Walk_Fwd_Loop`, `Zombie_Idle_Loop`, `Wounded_Walk_Loop`, `Hurt_Stomach_Walk_Loop` |\n| Overhead tool, hook swing, claw or grab, throw | `Chop_Wood`, `Melee_Hook`, `Zombie_Scratch`, `OverhandThrow` |\n| Stagger (KICK window), kneel (SLAM window) | `Dizzy`, `Kneeling Tired` |\n| Hit, thrown down, get up, die | `Hit_Chest`, `Hit_Head`, `Hit_Knockback`, `LayToIdle`, `Fall_On_Face`, `Get_Up_Face_Down`, `Death01`, `Death_A/B/C` |\n| Monk shield, Steward lunge | `Idle_Shield_Loop`, `Idle_Shield_Break`, `Sword_Dash_RM` |\n| Vera's kick | `Roundhouse_Kick`, `Side_Kick`, `Front_Kick` (windows only) |\n| Traversal | `Climb Ladder`, `ClimbUp_1m_RM`, `Run_Jump_Over`, `Land_Three_Point`, `Push_Loop`, `Crawl` (downed) |\n| Ambrose | `Walk_Formal_Loop`, `Idle_FoldArms_Loop`, `Defend`, `Duck_Thrown_Object`, `Drink_Water`, `Consume`, `Greeting` |\n| Carrying him | `Walk_Carry_Loop`, `Carry_Heavy_Box` |\n| Talk, banquet, balcony, lantern patrol | `Idle_Talking_Loop`, `Sitting_Idle_Loop`, `Sitting_Talking_Loop`, `Idle_Rail_Loop`, `Idle_Lantern_Loop`, `Looking_Around` |\n\n**New animation we must author** (a clip is text; see `engine/docs/MOCAP.md`; copy the nearest clip's key poses and edit the numbers, or add a procedural layer):\n- **SLAM:** Vera behind a kneeling enemy, hooks under the arms and throws them back over her hip. Two-body sync: paired clips, positions placed by script.\n- **FRISK:** a quick pat-down of a downed enemy that shakes loose crowns.\n- **Stomp and heel-grind** (finish a crawler or a downed enemy).\n- **Ambrose's tasting** (from `Consume`: sniff, swirl, sip, grimace) and **Ambrose carried** over a shoulder (a limp hung pose).\n- **Boost and catch** (two-body sync).\n- **The Treader's stomp and cask throw**, **the Comte's languid gestures** on the sedan chair, **Vane's transformation** (pieces drawn procedurally).\n- **The sabrage** at the finale.\n\nCheck each once with a strip of screenshots stepped by `game.advance` (§17).\n\n**Non-human bodies** (draw with the pen on a small procedural skeleton; the horror kit's `drawDog` is the pattern): truffle hog, Bloom crawler, Bloom tendril (a chain of tubes on a spring), the Comte's vine colossus, Vane's final form.\n\n**Hit zones.** For each enemy near the shot's line, build capsules from its pose: head (a sphere at the skull, about 3 units), torso (pelvis to neck, about 4), upper and lower arm, thigh and shin. The shot:\n1. `world.ray(ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz, range, { skip: [hero, ...enemies] })` finds what the level or props stop it at.\n2. Test the ray against the capsules, nearest first, up to that distance. The first capsule hit is the zone.\n\nKeep capsules a little fatter than the art at range, and let aim assist (§6) help the pad player.\n\n## 6. The player\n\n**Controls.** Bind them in `KEYS` with these actions and name them all for the engine's rebinding screen (`menu.controls`). DualSense standard mapping. Keyboard and mouse are what PC players expect, not stand-ins.\n\n| Action | DualSense | Keyboard / mouse |\n|---|---|---|\n| Move / look | Left stick / right stick | WASD / mouse (a click captures it) |\n| Aim (hold; feet planted) | L2 | Right mouse |\n| Fire | R2 | Left mouse (while aiming) |\n| Action: door, vault, ladder, pick up, talk, KICK, SLAM, prompts | Cross | E (also Space), or left mouse on the focused thing when not aiming |\n| Sprint (hold); sprint + Action kicks a door in | Circle (also Cancel in menus) | Shift |\n| Quick turn (180°) | Stick back + Cross | X, or S + Space |\n| Reload | Square | R |\n| Evidence case | Triangle | Tab or I |\n| Knife (a tap slashes) | L1 | F |","numLines":130,"startLine":300,"totalLines":994}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QZrVqBG4AjRHto8AC1ELPz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
