{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VqNBZoQyNoVnDwmJwdtvJi","type":"tool_result","content":"321865 latest/CHANGELOG.md\n1:# Changelog\n8:## 0.89.1 (2026-11-05)\n19:## 0.89.0 (2026-11-05)\n33:## 0.88.0 (2026-11-05)\n52:## 0.87.0 (2026-11-05)\n81:## 0.86.1 (2026-11-05)\n89:## 0.86.0 (2026-11-04)\n106:## 0.85.3 (2026-11-05)\n114:## 0.85.2 (2026-11-03)\n122:## 0.85.1 (2026-10-30)\n129:## 0.85.0 (2026-10-30)\n147:## 0.84.1 (2026-10-29)\n# Changelog\n\nOne version number covers the engine, its agent edition, the docs and the games (semantic versioning while the major number is 0: the minor number for features, the patch for fixes). Every change merged to `main` bumps it and adds its lines here (except a change confined to a prototype, a Temporary lab: `DOCTRINE.md`), sorted under the parts it touched: **Rendering**, **Animation**, **Art**, **Mocap**, **Engine** (the rest of it), **Emberdeep**, **Characters**, **Tools**, **Docs**.\n\n- `node tools/version.mjs 0.8.0` writes a new number everywhere it lives and opens a section here; `npm test` fails while any place disagrees or this file has no section for it.\n- Each deployed page carries its build, the commit it was deployed from: `My3D2dge.build` and `My3D2dge.versionLabel()` (`v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07`), shown on the Emberdeep title, in its Settings (Keys & help), on the Labs page and in the Mocap Lab. Copies built from the repo say `dev-build`.\n\n## 0.89.1 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan is done: its last two items, prototypes, are Temporary labs for the owner to judge, and the plan is gone (its text: `git show 5f44010:docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`; what wasn't taken: F8 in `docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md`).\n\n- **Rendering**: the 3D camera's roll (`camera.fixed`'s, and the behind camera's share of a flyer's) leans as `pen.at`'s does, its right side down: it leaned the other way, so the flight kit's camera leaned against the ship's bank.\n- **Tools**: the Gallery Lab (`/gallery-lab`, the plan's 3.4): a light-gun gallery on the mode7 module, with `m7.quad` (world polygons sorted with the billboards) and `m7.pick` (what was drawn under the crosshair) put on its prototype: are they the module's, or does a 2D game need a perspective `E.View`?\n- **Tools**: the Track Lab (`/track-lab`, 7.5): a ribbon track on the 3D engine (a loop, banked bends), its frames as data (`track.at(s)`, `track.toTrack(x, y, z)`) and a craft in its space: is a track the engine's, and in what form?\n- **Tools**: the Fork Lab (`/fork-lab`, 7.5): a pseudo-3D road that forks, the side the car is on choosing the way, several roads drawn in one pass: is a fork `E.Road`'s own (`road.fork`), or a game's?\n- **Tools**: kit3d-test checks a fixed camera's roll against the pen's (their up vectors agree).\n- **Docs**: `docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md` deleted, its items landed in v0.86.0 to v0.89.1 or logged (F8.1 to F8.10); the README and F8's entry say where it went; the 3D header says a fixed camera's roll is `pen.at`'s.\n\n## 0.89.0 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan's third batch (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, their lines gone): the Humanoid's registries (Part 4), sound (Part 5) and menus and the Shell for a TV (Part 6). Each was reproduced first in the engine's own suites.\n\n- **Characters**: the Humanoid's registries: `E.Humanoid.weapon(id, { length, rest, blade, draw(w) })`, `E.Humanoid.hat(id, draw(h))` and `E.Humanoid.pose(id, (J, w, rig) => ...)` (a held pose of the game's own, eased in and out by `state.pose` over the rig's solve); `E.Humanoid.weapons` and `hats` hold the built-ins, moved onto them: the sword, gun and staff in both styles and the five hats draw the same pixels as before (filmed in five views with swings, against the old build: identical). 37 builds changed the rig for weapons, hats, outfits and poses. The built-in poses stay the rig's own solve (F8.10).\n- **Characters**: `new E.Humanoid({ draw: { under(g, Q, rig), over(g, Q, rig) } })`: a game's own drawing before and after the body, with the joints on screen (outfits, accessories); `state.tilt` and `state.roll`: the whole body tilted forward (a dive, a fall) or rolled sideways, about the feet.\n- **Characters**: a rig draws from its own random stream (`{ seed }`, else one draw of `Math.random` as it is made): its blinks never draw from `Math.random`, so they move no game's stream (a headless run's rigs, the SIM's crowd), and a seed gives the same rig twice. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: `audio.sfx(name, { pan, loop })` returns its handle, `{ stop(fade), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }` (a handle that does nothing when nothing plays: headless, before the first key): a looped effect sounds on until stopped (an engine, rain), through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute, and starts while muted; `audio.instrument(name, (ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) => ...)` is a wave a music track or a voice takes (9 builds extended the synth).\n- **Engine**: `new E.Menu(game, items, { scale, rows })`: its text and rows that many times (a TV across a room), and at most that many entries shown, the list scrolling under its cursor (▲ ▼ when there are more; a click or tap picks a shown entry; `rowAt(i)` is null for one scrolled away). At scale 1 with every row shown it draws as before.\n- **Engine**: `new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale } })` (a number, or a function of the window) or `{ tv: true }` (by the window's width): the whole interface that many times, still filling the window (its text was 14 px); a font shorthand in `theme.font` warns (it is a family; two runs' menus came out tiny); a screen of the game's own lists setting rows, `rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading']`, changed as on the settings screen (8 builds).\n- **Tools**: the contract suite holds the registries (a game's weapon, hat and pose drawn in both styles, its tip at its length; the built-ins on the registries), the draw hooks, the tilt and roll, the rig's own stream (counted on the engine's own `Math.random`), the effect's handle headless and the menu's scale and rows; the audio suite's part 6 (a looped, panned effect sounds until stopped, its pan set as it plays; a track plays a game's own instrument); the standard suite a TV's Shell (setting rows on a screen of the game's own, a theme scale, a font shorthand warned).\n- **Tools**: the 3D RACING kit's bot slows for the turn into the dirt shortcut (off a boost it ran past it every lap: the rigs' own random stream changed the race's items, and with them its speed there); its note on the rigs says they draw one `Math.random` each.\n- **Docs**: the API card names the registries, the draw hooks, tilt and roll, the effect's handle and loops, instruments, the menu's scale and rows, the Shell's scale and rows, and what the 5x7 font draws (anything else is `?`); D81 (the card's budget 21,300 and the six kits' own 800 higher: Parts 4 to 6 past what cutting could pay); F8.10; D82 (the API index's budget 64,000, the owner's word: its doc comments stay as written).\n\n## 0.88.0 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan's second batch (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, their lines gone): more input (1.5 to 1.9), and the 2D renderer's floors, sprites, spans, flow fields and colours (3.1 to 3.3, 3.7 to 3.11). Each was reproduced first in the engine's own suites.\n\n- **Engine**: `input.value(a)`: how far an action is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, short of a press too), a stick's direction by its push, any other code held 1 (8 builds patched analog triggers in).\n- **Engine**: the right stick is codes to bind, `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` (past halfway), and the mouse wheel `WheelUp`/`WheelDown` (a notch taps it; a bound wheel doesn't zoom), named ('R stick ←', 'Wheel up') and rebindable on the Controls screen.\n- **Engine**: `new E.Game({ touch: 'never' })`: no touch controls ever and no Touch settings; on a device with touch alone a notice says a controller or a keyboard is needed, with a Settings button, until one is connected or a key pressed.\n- **Engine**: `game.hook('input' | 'step' | 'enter', fn)` returns its removal: 'input' every step, paused too (a host's guard, an idle timer), 'step' in play, 'enter' with the scene's name and data (8 builds exported the engine's private hooks).\n- **Engine**: the Controller settings invert the move stick (Invert move left and right, up and down), beside the aim's; a player who joins takes them.\n- **Rendering**: a tile map's floor follows a smooth zoom without a bake a frame: while the zoom moves (a camera framing two heroes, a game's own setZoom each frame) the last bake of that view's angle is drawn scaled, crisp again once the zoom holds a quarter second or drifts past 1.25 times; the bakes are held to `map.floorBudget` bytes (64 MB, `floorBytes()`), the least recently used let go (it kept seven).\n- **Rendering**: `r.ppu`: pixels per world unit (the view's scale times the zoom: iso 1.41, the others 1.5, overhead 1); `r.sprite(..., { world: true })` grows a sprite with the view, as rigs and maps (a sprite's pixel is a screen pixel at zoom 1).\n- **Rendering**: `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn, { depth })` queues a long wall, fence or roof's edge a cell at a time, each piece sorted at its own middle, clipped to its part, with its box for the x-ray (one point can't sort a long thing against what stands along it); `r.queue`'s `box` and `top` are documented (an occluder without a box joins the x-ray only round its foot).\n- **Engine**: a flow field walks round `map.solid` shapes (a crate standing on the floor raises the cells under it to its top: agents went through them), and `flow.update([[x, y], ...])` leads every cell to the nearest of several targets (the hero a horde chases).\n- **Engine**: colours: `rgb()`, `rgba()`, `hsl()`, `hsla()` and the basic CSS names ('orange', 'navy', 'white') are colours where the engine reads one (they drew magenta); `px` takes `E.hex`'s `[r, g, b]` too. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: the road module's `square: true`: one focal length, so a unit across is a unit up on a screen of any shape; `road.ahead(h, r)` gives the camera's distance behind a car at the screen's foot on that screen. The default and the ROAD kit are as they were (F8.9).\n- **Engine**: what a game opts into stays out of a kit whose game never calls it, in sections of the diet: `input.value`, `game.hook` and the touch-free notice (10l), `r.queueSpan` (9b); a scene field's warning is shorter.\n- **Tools**: the contract suite holds each (a trigger's travel and a stick's push, the right stick and the wheel bound and rebound, touch: 'never', the hooks paused and in play, the move stick inverted, a smooth zoom's bakes, the byte budget, `r.ppu` and a world sprite, a span's pieces, flow fields round crates and to two targets, CSS colours, a square road and its foot); the phone test's touchless case (`tools/fixtures/touchless.html`: no buttons, the notice, its Settings, gone at a key).\n- **Docs**: the API card's Coordinates say each view's pixels per unit, and it names `value`, the right stick's and the wheel's codes, `touch: 'never'`, `hook`, the move stick's settings, `queueSpan`, a flow field's targets and solids, and the colours (its standard features and touch lines shorter, for room); D80 (the six kits' own budgets 1,500 tokens higher: Part 0's room went to the core's fixes); F8.9 (the road square by default: the owner's call on the kit's look).\n\n## 0.87.0 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan's first batch (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, their lines gone): input for a shared world (1.1 to 1.4, 1.10; 1.2 a call of its own, D78), headless tests that see what a player sees (Part 2; 2.6 a call, D79: 13 builders found `draw` never ran headless, 6 shipped a draw-time crash their suites passed), the mode7 module's shapes and views (3.5, 3.6), the 3D engine's filtered first person and stacked split screens (7.1 to 7.4), and the card's Common mistakes (Part 8).\n\n- **Engine**: Create + Options is the host's (the AI Hub opens its corner bar on it): Options (`Pad9`) while Create (`Pad8`) is held is no press, so no pause, no Start and no join on a free pad (20 builds patched it); alone, Options pauses as before. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: `new E.Game({ live: true })` and `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`: the menus open over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player); `game.paused` says a menu is up, the scene's update goes on, the menu holds that player's input back.\n- **Engine**: `input.use(preset)` keeps the keys held: down in the new layout, not pressed again, and a press no step has seen yet counts in it (it let go of every key, so a key held into a scene with its own `input` read up until pressed again, and a press on the step a layout changed was lost). Both editions.\n- **Engine**: a preset's own halves for two on one keyboard: `preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } }` gives those actions their keys (their pads kept); an action a menu reads beside `confirm` took confirm's F. `use()` takes such a preset. DEFAULT's halves are as before.\n- **Engine**: the arcade module's attract mode wakes on any player's input and on a player joining (it heard player 1 only).\n- **Engine**: `new E.Game({ headless: true, draw: true })` draws a frame each `advance` on a stand-in screen (in Node, one whose drawing does nothing; canvas lighting skips its pixels there), so a draw error lands in `game.errors`. The handoff package's template game is made with it, so `node game/test.mjs` draws too.\n- **Engine**: `E.Menu` lays itself out in `update` from the screen's size: a click or tap picks an entry with no draw yet (its rows came from a draw: scripted clicks missed in 9 builds); `menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box on the screen, for a test's click.\n- **Engine**: timers made before the first scene live into it (a deep link's timer was cleared by the first scene's entry); a boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends (it warned \"needs game.start() first\" and the first scene started). Both editions.\n- **Engine**: `E.store` keeps a value for the visit where there is no `localStorage` or it is blocked or full (Node, a sandboxed frame such as the AI Hub's: nothing was kept, 16 builds); `set` still returns false then. `E.store.clear()` forgets every key, for tests that make several games in one process. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: a scene's fields the engine reads (`view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `camera`), of the wrong type, are left alone and warn once, naming the field and what it should be: a method named `camera()` was taken for the scene's camera options and hid the game's (Gauntletdown's zoom never applied). Both editions (the agent edition reads the first five).\n- **Engine**: `game.timeScale` outlives a scene change and a hit-stop doesn't, as before, now a call (D79): a game's speed (Emberdeep's, the SIM kit's) lives across scenes, and a slow motion's restore is a kept timer; the index and the handoff README say so.\n- **Engine**: the boot screen's finish waits for a page: a headless game that draws has none (it called `clearInterval`, which a bare Node context lacks).\n- **Engine**: the ADVENTURE slice's relic sparkles from its update (its draw spawned the glints with `Math.random`, so drawing changed the game); the SIM slice culls its guests' rigs by the camera, not the last frame's offset (none headless, so the headless proof wasn't the page's).\n- **Engine**: the mode7 module's `m7.shape(id, x, y, z, { params, mats })` draws a shape's params and materials, each look a sprite of its own (they were dropped, and the cache didn't tell them apart); a camera whose pitch bobs by a fraction of a degree reuses its views (whole degrees: 120 frames made 120 views and 120 shape rasterizations).\n- **Rendering**: the 3D engine's filters (cel, pixel, bloom) and a first-person overlay: the overlay is drawn inside the filter's pass, over the filtered picture where its own depth says it drew; after the pass, three r182 copied a stale buffer over it, and the picture went black but for the gun.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"23de6e6b-3ae6-4bf5-9764-44b18bb5857c","uuid":"724a182b-1705-4079-a05a-26da3cc84608","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:31:40.541Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"321865 latest/CHANGELOG.md\n1:# Changelog\n8:## 0.89.1 (2026-11-05)\n19:## 0.89.0 (2026-11-05)\n33:## 0.88.0 (2026-11-05)\n52:## 0.87.0 (2026-11-05)\n81:## 0.86.1 (2026-11-05)\n89:## 0.86.0 (2026-11-04)\n106:## 0.85.3 (2026-11-05)\n114:## 0.85.2 (2026-11-03)\n122:## 0.85.1 (2026-10-30)\n129:## 0.85.0 (2026-10-30)\n147:## 0.84.1 (2026-10-29)\n# Changelog\n\nOne version number covers the engine, its agent edition, the docs and the games (semantic versioning while the major number is 0: the minor number for features, the patch for fixes). Every change merged to `main` bumps it and adds its lines here (except a change confined to a prototype, a Temporary lab: `DOCTRINE.md`), sorted under the parts it touched: **Rendering**, **Animation**, **Art**, **Mocap**, **Engine** (the rest of it), **Emberdeep**, **Characters**, **Tools**, **Docs**.\n\n- `node tools/version.mjs 0.8.0` writes a new number everywhere it lives and opens a section here; `npm test` fails while any place disagrees or this file has no section for it.\n- Each deployed page carries its build, the commit it was deployed from: `My3D2dge.build` and `My3D2dge.versionLabel()` (`v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07`), shown on the Emberdeep title, in its Settings (Keys & help), on the Labs page and in the Mocap Lab. Copies built from the repo say `dev-build`.\n\n## 0.89.1 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan is done: its last two items, prototypes, are Temporary labs for the owner to judge, and the plan is gone (its text: `git show 5f44010:docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`; what wasn't taken: F8 in `docs/FEEDBACK-LOG.md`).\n\n- **Rendering**: the 3D camera's roll (`camera.fixed`'s, and the behind camera's share of a flyer's) leans as `pen.at`'s does, its right side down: it leaned the other way, so the flight kit's camera leaned against the ship's bank.\n- **Tools**: the Gallery Lab (`/gallery-lab`, the plan's 3.4): a light-gun gallery on the mode7 module, with `m7.quad` (world polygons sorted with the billboards) and `m7.pick` (what was drawn under the crosshair) put on its prototype: are they the module's, or does a 2D game need a perspective `E.View`?\n- **Tools**: the Track Lab (`/track-lab`, 7.5): a ribbon track on the 3D engine (a loop, banked bends), its frames as data (`track.at(s)`, `track.toTrack(x, y, z)`) and a craft in its space: is a track the engine's, and in what form?\n- **Tools**: the Fork Lab (`/fork-lab`, 7.5): a pseudo-3D road that forks, the side the car is on choosing the way, several roads drawn in one pass: is a fork `E.Road`'s own (`road.fork`), or a game's?\n- **Tools**: kit3d-test checks a fixed camera's roll against the pen's (their up vectors agree).\n- **Docs**: `docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md` deleted, its items landed in v0.86.0 to v0.89.1 or logged (F8.1 to F8.10); the README and F8's entry say where it went; the 3D header says a fixed camera's roll is `pen.at`'s.\n\n## 0.89.0 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan's third batch (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, their lines gone): the Humanoid's registries (Part 4), sound (Part 5) and menus and the Shell for a TV (Part 6). Each was reproduced first in the engine's own suites.\n\n- **Characters**: the Humanoid's registries: `E.Humanoid.weapon(id, { length, rest, blade, draw(w) })`, `E.Humanoid.hat(id, draw(h))` and `E.Humanoid.pose(id, (J, w, rig) => ...)` (a held pose of the game's own, eased in and out by `state.pose` over the rig's solve); `E.Humanoid.weapons` and `hats` hold the built-ins, moved onto them: the sword, gun and staff in both styles and the five hats draw the same pixels as before (filmed in five views with swings, against the old build: identical). 37 builds changed the rig for weapons, hats, outfits and poses. The built-in poses stay the rig's own solve (F8.10).\n- **Characters**: `new E.Humanoid({ draw: { under(g, Q, rig), over(g, Q, rig) } })`: a game's own drawing before and after the body, with the joints on screen (outfits, accessories); `state.tilt` and `state.roll`: the whole body tilted forward (a dive, a fall) or rolled sideways, about the feet.\n- **Characters**: a rig draws from its own random stream (`{ seed }`, else one draw of `Math.random` as it is made): its blinks never draw from `Math.random`, so they move no game's stream (a headless run's rigs, the SIM's crowd), and a seed gives the same rig twice. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: `audio.sfx(name, { pan, loop })` returns its handle, `{ stop(fade), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }` (a handle that does nothing when nothing plays: headless, before the first key): a looped effect sounds on until stopped (an engine, rain), through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute, and starts while muted; `audio.instrument(name, (ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) => ...)` is a wave a music track or a voice takes (9 builds extended the synth).\n- **Engine**: `new E.Menu(game, items, { scale, rows })`: its text and rows that many times (a TV across a room), and at most that many entries shown, the list scrolling under its cursor (▲ ▼ when there are more; a click or tap picks a shown entry; `rowAt(i)` is null for one scrolled away). At scale 1 with every row shown it draws as before.\n- **Engine**: `new E.Shell(game, { theme: { scale } })` (a number, or a function of the window) or `{ tv: true }` (by the window's width): the whole interface that many times, still filling the window (its text was 14 px); a font shorthand in `theme.font` warns (it is a family; two runs' menus came out tiny); a screen of the game's own lists setting rows, `rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading']`, changed as on the settings screen (8 builds).\n- **Tools**: the contract suite holds the registries (a game's weapon, hat and pose drawn in both styles, its tip at its length; the built-ins on the registries), the draw hooks, the tilt and roll, the rig's own stream (counted on the engine's own `Math.random`), the effect's handle headless and the menu's scale and rows; the audio suite's part 6 (a looped, panned effect sounds until stopped, its pan set as it plays; a track plays a game's own instrument); the standard suite a TV's Shell (setting rows on a screen of the game's own, a theme scale, a font shorthand warned).\n- **Tools**: the 3D RACING kit's bot slows for the turn into the dirt shortcut (off a boost it ran past it every lap: the rigs' own random stream changed the race's items, and with them its speed there); its note on the rigs says they draw one `Math.random` each.\n- **Docs**: the API card names the registries, the draw hooks, tilt and roll, the effect's handle and loops, instruments, the menu's scale and rows, the Shell's scale and rows, and what the 5x7 font draws (anything else is `?`); D81 (the card's budget 21,300 and the six kits' own 800 higher: Parts 4 to 6 past what cutting could pay); F8.10; D82 (the API index's budget 64,000, the owner's word: its doc comments stay as written).\n\n## 0.88.0 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan's second batch (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, their lines gone): more input (1.5 to 1.9), and the 2D renderer's floors, sprites, spans, flow fields and colours (3.1 to 3.3, 3.7 to 3.11). Each was reproduced first in the engine's own suites.\n\n- **Engine**: `input.value(a)`: how far an action is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, short of a press too), a stick's direction by its push, any other code held 1 (8 builds patched analog triggers in).\n- **Engine**: the right stick is codes to bind, `PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right` (past halfway), and the mouse wheel `WheelUp`/`WheelDown` (a notch taps it; a bound wheel doesn't zoom), named ('R stick ←', 'Wheel up') and rebindable on the Controls screen.\n- **Engine**: `new E.Game({ touch: 'never' })`: no touch controls ever and no Touch settings; on a device with touch alone a notice says a controller or a keyboard is needed, with a Settings button, until one is connected or a key pressed.\n- **Engine**: `game.hook('input' | 'step' | 'enter', fn)` returns its removal: 'input' every step, paused too (a host's guard, an idle timer), 'step' in play, 'enter' with the scene's name and data (8 builds exported the engine's private hooks).\n- **Engine**: the Controller settings invert the move stick (Invert move left and right, up and down), beside the aim's; a player who joins takes them.\n- **Rendering**: a tile map's floor follows a smooth zoom without a bake a frame: while the zoom moves (a camera framing two heroes, a game's own setZoom each frame) the last bake of that view's angle is drawn scaled, crisp again once the zoom holds a quarter second or drifts past 1.25 times; the bakes are held to `map.floorBudget` bytes (64 MB, `floorBytes()`), the least recently used let go (it kept seven).\n- **Rendering**: `r.ppu`: pixels per world unit (the view's scale times the zoom: iso 1.41, the others 1.5, overhead 1); `r.sprite(..., { world: true })` grows a sprite with the view, as rigs and maps (a sprite's pixel is a screen pixel at zoom 1).\n- **Rendering**: `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn, { depth })` queues a long wall, fence or roof's edge a cell at a time, each piece sorted at its own middle, clipped to its part, with its box for the x-ray (one point can't sort a long thing against what stands along it); `r.queue`'s `box` and `top` are documented (an occluder without a box joins the x-ray only round its foot).\n- **Engine**: a flow field walks round `map.solid` shapes (a crate standing on the floor raises the cells under it to its top: agents went through them), and `flow.update([[x, y], ...])` leads every cell to the nearest of several targets (the hero a horde chases).\n- **Engine**: colours: `rgb()`, `rgba()`, `hsl()`, `hsla()` and the basic CSS names ('orange', 'navy', 'white') are colours where the engine reads one (they drew magenta); `px` takes `E.hex`'s `[r, g, b]` too. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: the road module's `square: true`: one focal length, so a unit across is a unit up on a screen of any shape; `road.ahead(h, r)` gives the camera's distance behind a car at the screen's foot on that screen. The default and the ROAD kit are as they were (F8.9).\n- **Engine**: what a game opts into stays out of a kit whose game never calls it, in sections of the diet: `input.value`, `game.hook` and the touch-free notice (10l), `r.queueSpan` (9b); a scene field's warning is shorter.\n- **Tools**: the contract suite holds each (a trigger's travel and a stick's push, the right stick and the wheel bound and rebound, touch: 'never', the hooks paused and in play, the move stick inverted, a smooth zoom's bakes, the byte budget, `r.ppu` and a world sprite, a span's pieces, flow fields round crates and to two targets, CSS colours, a square road and its foot); the phone test's touchless case (`tools/fixtures/touchless.html`: no buttons, the notice, its Settings, gone at a key).\n- **Docs**: the API card's Coordinates say each view's pixels per unit, and it names `value`, the right stick's and the wheel's codes, `touch: 'never'`, `hook`, the move stick's settings, `queueSpan`, a flow field's targets and solids, and the colours (its standard features and touch lines shorter, for room); D80 (the six kits' own budgets 1,500 tokens higher: Part 0's room went to the core's fixes); F8.9 (the road square by default: the owner's call on the kit's look).\n\n## 0.87.0 (2026-11-05)\n\nThe cc-matrix plan's first batch (`docs/HANDOFF-MATRIX.md`, their lines gone): input for a shared world (1.1 to 1.4, 1.10; 1.2 a call of its own, D78), headless tests that see what a player sees (Part 2; 2.6 a call, D79: 13 builders found `draw` never ran headless, 6 shipped a draw-time crash their suites passed), the mode7 module's shapes and views (3.5, 3.6), the 3D engine's filtered first person and stacked split screens (7.1 to 7.4), and the card's Common mistakes (Part 8).\n\n- **Engine**: Create + Options is the host's (the AI Hub opens its corner bar on it): Options (`Pad9`) while Create (`Pad8`) is held is no press, so no pause, no Start and no join on a free pad (20 builds patched it); alone, Options pauses as before. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: `new E.Game({ live: true })` and `game.menu(name, { pause: false })`: the menus open over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player); `game.paused` says a menu is up, the scene's update goes on, the menu holds that player's input back.\n- **Engine**: `input.use(preset)` keeps the keys held: down in the new layout, not pressed again, and a press no step has seen yet counts in it (it let go of every key, so a key held into a scene with its own `input` read up until pressed again, and a press on the step a layout changed was lost). Both editions.\n- **Engine**: a preset's own halves for two on one keyboard: `preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } }` gives those actions their keys (their pads kept); an action a menu reads beside `confirm` took confirm's F. `use()` takes such a preset. DEFAULT's halves are as before.\n- **Engine**: the arcade module's attract mode wakes on any player's input and on a player joining (it heard player 1 only).\n- **Engine**: `new E.Game({ headless: true, draw: true })` draws a frame each `advance` on a stand-in screen (in Node, one whose drawing does nothing; canvas lighting skips its pixels there), so a draw error lands in `game.errors`. The handoff package's template game is made with it, so `node game/test.mjs` draws too.\n- **Engine**: `E.Menu` lays itself out in `update` from the screen's size: a click or tap picks an entry with no draw yet (its rows came from a draw: scripted clicks missed in 9 builds); `menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box on the screen, for a test's click.\n- **Engine**: timers made before the first scene live into it (a deep link's timer was cleared by the first scene's entry); a boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends (it warned \"needs game.start() first\" and the first scene started). Both editions.\n- **Engine**: `E.store` keeps a value for the visit where there is no `localStorage` or it is blocked or full (Node, a sandboxed frame such as the AI Hub's: nothing was kept, 16 builds); `set` still returns false then. `E.store.clear()` forgets every key, for tests that make several games in one process. Both editions.\n- **Engine**: a scene's fields the engine reads (`view`, `views`, `input`, `res`, `expand`, `touch`, `camera`), of the wrong type, are left alone and warn once, naming the field and what it should be: a method named `camera()` was taken for the scene's camera options and hid the game's (Gauntletdown's zoom never applied). Both editions (the agent edition reads the first five).\n- **Engine**: `game.timeScale` outlives a scene change and a hit-stop doesn't, as before, now a call (D79): a game's speed (Emberdeep's, the SIM kit's) lives across scenes, and a slow motion's restore is a kept timer; the index and the handoff README say so.\n- **Engine**: the boot screen's finish waits for a page: a headless game that draws has none (it called `clearInterval`, which a bare Node context lacks).\n- **Engine**: the ADVENTURE slice's relic sparkles from its update (its draw spawned the glints with `Math.random`, so drawing changed the game); the SIM slice culls its guests' rigs by the camera, not the last frame's offset (none headless, so the headless proof wasn't the page's).\n- **Engine**: the mode7 module's `m7.shape(id, x, y, z, { params, mats })` draws a shape's params and materials, each look a sprite of its own (they were dropped, and the cache didn't tell them apart); a camera whose pitch bobs by a fraction of a degree reuses its views (whole degrees: 120 frames made 120 views and 120 shape rasterizations).\n- **Rendering**: the 3D engine's filters (cel, pixel, bloom) and a first-person overlay: the overlay is drawn inside the filter's pass, over the filtered picture where its own depth says it drew; after the pass, three r182 copied a stale buffer over it, and the picture went black but for the gun.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VqNBZoQyNoVnDwmJwdtvJi","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
